  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n***Thursday***\n\nGEFS and EC 500-mb height ensembles still show a broad \nhigh/ridge across much of the United States Thursday morning. At\nthe same time, a shortwave trough traveling around the ridge \nanticyclonically is forecast to be overhead. A surface low at \nthe leading edge of the shortwave will allow a cold front to \ntraverse the Great Plains throughout the day. Showers and \nthunderstorms are possible at the leading edge of the cold \nfront, though are currently not expected to be severe. LREF \nguidance suggests only a 25% chance or less that sfc-500mb wind \nshear will exceed 30 kts, with the highest chances in Southwest \nNebraska and along US-283 in Kansas. GFS and EC model soundings \nsupport this solution, showing less than 30 kts of 0-6 km wind \nshear in this zone. However, latest GFS and NAMNST model \nsoundings do show an inverted-V up to about 3 km, which may \nindicate microburst potential with storms. Confidence in storm \ncoverage is low at this time, as NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance suggests only up to a 1 in 3 chance to receive greater \nthan 0.1 inches of rain across the forecast area. Confidence in \nsevere weather occurring is around 5% Thursday.\n\n***Friday/Saturday***\n\nThursday's cold front looks to begin eroding the strong ridge \nacross the Great Plains, moving the ridge axis westward over the\nFour- Corners Region by Friday morning. Flow at 500-mb over the\nforecast region is favored to be northwesterly to northerly \nthrough Saturday. As such, highs are forecast to slightly drop \ninto the low to mid 90s Friday, and upper 80s to lower 90s \nSaturday. A surface high in association with the ridge looks to \nbe to the north of the forecast area, and may establish \nsoutherly winds across Eastern Colorado Friday evening, with \neasterly flow across Western Kansas and Southwest Nebraska. As \nsuch, showers may develop across Eastern Colorado Friday. Again,\ncoverage is uncertain at this time, as NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance suggests less than a 25% chance for greater than 0.1 \ninches of rain across most of this zone. However, strengthening \nof the shortwave trough to the east may support the development \nof a 500-mb jet streak over the region. This is reflected in \nLREF sfc-500mb wind shear guidance, which shows a 40-60% chance \nof wind shear greater than 30 kts across Eastern Colorado. If \nthunderstorms are able to take advantage of this, they may be \nallowed to become severe. Even so, confidence in severe weather \nFriday evening is about 5% due to uncertainty in the coverage.\n\n***Sunday-Tuesday***\n\nThe ridge axis is favored to be overhead once again Sunday as a\nstrong 500-mb trough traversing Southern Canada and the \nNorthern United States pushes it east. Conditions would warm up \nagain into the mid and upper 90s Sunday, and upper 90s to lower \n100s Monday. However, conditions look to be drier. GEFS 850-mb \nguidance shows a weak low in the U.S. Southeast. This would \ncreate northerly flow across the Texas coasts and prevent \nmoisture from returning to the forecast region. Relative \nhumidities (RH) may drop into the lower 20s to upper teens \nduring this period. While this does not meet critical fire \nweather criteria (RH<15%), NBM guidance shows a broad region \nacross Eastern Colorado and Southwest Nebraska with a 50% chance\nor higher of experiencing wind gusts over 30 mph both Sunday \nand Monday. Considering that fuels are drier across this region,\nthe potential development of critical fire weather conditions \nmay be something to keep an eye on going into the weekend. \nAdditionally, a surface low associated with a trough to the \nnorth may produce a few showers along a cold front Monday and \nTuesday.",
